# Development happens on Windows, where core.autocrlf is normally on. Shell
# scripts must reach the Linux CI runners with LF endings regardless of any
# contributor's local git config: bash reads a CRLF script as commands ending
# in a stray \r and fails with "command not found". Pin them explicitly rather
# than relying on autocrlf normalising the blob.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Same reasoning for the workflow files: YAML tolerates CRLF, but the shell
# fragments inside `run:` blocks are extracted verbatim and run by bash.
*.yml text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf

# rustfmt normalises to LF regardless of platform; pin the blob the same way so
# a Windows checkout's autocrlf can't disagree with what `cargo fmt --check`
# (rust/, roadmap AP9) expects in CI.
*.rs text eol=lf

# The three products' notice fragments, licence texts and platform files (notices/) are read at
# configure time and concatenated into each package's NOTICES.txt (cmake/Notices.cmake); the
# generator normalises line endings, and pinning the blobs to LF keeps the checked-in bytes the same
# on every platform too. Without it a Windows checkout would be CRLF for files that are
# concatenated together.
notices/** text eol=lf

# The ESP32 player's web page. The component embeds these files in firmware
# byte for byte (esp-idf/iclforge/CMakeLists.txt, EMBED_FILES), and the host
# tests hold them to a size budget and map coverage onto their offsets, so all
# three need the same bytes on every checkout.
esp-idf/iclforge/ui/** text eol=lf

# vcpkg overlay ports. vcpkg applies a port's patches to upstream's sources, which have
# LF endings, so a patch that a Windows checkout had turned to CRLF would add stray
# carriage returns or fail to apply.
cmake/vcpkg/ports/** text eol=lf

# ESP-IDF example stream assets. Binary: git must not touch line endings in an
# elementary bitstream, where 0x0D 0x0A is audio data like any other pair of
# bytes - see the fixture-hash note above for the same failure caught once
# already on a text fixture.
*.ac3 -text
# And E-AC-3, for the same reason: the streaming example's stream set
# (esp-idf/iclforge/examples/hearth_sink/www/) is several dozen of them.
*.ec3 -text
*.ec3 -text
# And AC-4: the DEE streams under tests/golden/external-baseline/ that the
# decoder's syntax digests are taken over.
*.ac4 -text

# The hand-written part of stage S2 (tools/n1b/README.md), applied with git apply --3way: a patch that a
# Windows checkout had turned to CRLF would not apply to the LF blobs it was made from.
tools/n1b/s2-hand.patch text eol=lf
